DATA INFORMATION

1. OVERVIEW:
    The first version of this dataset has been used to conduct a quantitative 
    analysis of the CSI to gain insight into the potential and limitations of 
    CSI-based ambient sensing in the paper "Towards a Quantitative Analysis of
    CSI for AI/ML Based Sensing." 

    This version of the dataset has been used in the extensions of the aforementioned
    work. 

    The dataset is split into several folders containing pcap and mat files, one 
    for each CSI capture considered in the paper. A detailed description 
    of all the experimental setups can be found in the paper, together 
    with a map of the environment reporting the positions of all the nodes.

2. CHANGES W.R.T. VERSION 1
    a. Folder naming has been converted from DDMMYYYY to YYYY-MM-DD with the
	optional addition of _HH-mm-SS, if known
    b. config_info.json files were renamed to config.json
    c. Additional fields were added to config.json:
	- code: indicates the experiment code
	- posTX, posRX: indicate the position of the transmitter and receiver in the room 
	- nframes: number of expected frames for each experiment in the set having 
		   the given configuration file
    d. The field "avgDuration" was removed from config.json
    e. CSI captures that were already present in V1 were converted from csv to mat files
    f. New CSI captures are already shared as one mat file per each antenna used within
	the same experiment + the pcap file from which they were extracted 

3. File naming convention:
    Every file is named according to the following naming convention:
        ./captureIDX.pcap
        ./captureIDX[coreN].mat
    where:
    - IDX identifies the index of the experiment within the selected 
    experimental setup.
    - coreN is optional and identifies the antenna from which the CSI were extracted.
    The used antennas are:
	Antenna 0 -> core1
	Antenna 2 -> core3
	Antenna 3 -> core4
    If coreN is missing, the implied value is core1.

    A complete description of the considered experimental setups can be found in the paper
    associated to version 10.5281/zenodo.17225694 of the dataset.


4. Dataset structure:
    A summarized representation of the structure of the dataset is presented below:

    |- README.md
    |- 20MHz
        |- 0people
            |- 2024-07-24
                |- capture0.pcap
                |- capture0.mat
                |- ...
                |- config.json
            |- 2024-07-12
                |- capture0.pcap
                |- capture0.mat
                |- ...
        |- 1people
            |- ...
        |- 4people
    |- 40MHz
        |- 0people
        |- ...
        |- 5people
            |- 2024-08-01
                |- S1
                    |- capture0.pcap
                    |- capture0.mat
                    |- ...
                |- S2
                    |- capture0.pcap
                    |- capture0.mat
                    |- ...
    |- 80MHz
        |- 0people
	    |- 2024-08-02
		|- capture0.pcap
		|- capture0.mat
		|- config.json
	    |- 2025-07-22_15-27-50
		|- capture1.pcap
		|- config.json
		|- capture1core1.mat
		|- capture1core3.mat
		|- ...
        |- ...

    The datasets consists of this README and some zipped folders (20MHz, 40MHz, and 80MHz), 
    each corresponding to a different channel bandwidth used during one or more experiments.
    The 80MHz folder had to be split into portions, each containing the CSI collected with
    a different number of people in the room.
    The 20 and 40MHz folders contain sub-folders named after the number of people in the room
    when the experiment was carried out.  

    Within each sub-folder is an additional folder named after the date of the experiment
    (YYYY-MM-DD[_HH-mm-SS]):
    -   If all experiments were performed under the same environmental conditions, such 
        sub-folders contain the pcap files (i.e., the raw CSI data), the extracted CSI data in mat
        files and the 'config.json' file with the description of the experimental setup (see 
	below for further details);
    -   If multiple experimental setups were used during the same day and HH-mm-SS is unknown,
	there is one additional sub-folder for each setup, which then contains the captures 
	and the config file.

    The config file contains some fields whose values are deemed relevant for the 
    identification of the experimental setup. Besides the straightforward fields (e.g. day, 
    month, year, ...), some additional ones are:
    - locationID: 
        * U004: Office 4, DII, University of Brescia (via Branze 38, Brescia)
        * L001: Telecommunication Laboratory, DII, University of Brescia (via Branze 38, Brescia)
    - experiment:
        * capture
    - adHocTransmission:
        * true: frames with random content are generated purposefully for the experiment
        * false: real traffic is captured
    - usleep: time in microseconds between two consecutive frames
    - nframes: number of expected frames for each experiment associated to this config file
    - bandwidth:
        * 20 [MHz]
        * 40 [MHz]
        * 80 [MHz]
    - modulation:
        * ax
        * ac
    - people
        * present:
            * true: people are present in the location during the capture
            * false: nobody is present in the location during the capture
        * moving:
            * true: people are present in the location and moving around 
            * false: people are either present and standing still or absent from the location
    - code: code identifying the experiments associated with this config file

    The names of the people inside the room (if any) have been anonymized.

METHODOLOGICAL INFORMATION

1. Data collection:
    All the CSI in the dataset have been collected using Nexmon CSI
    (https://github.com/seemoo-lab/nexmon_csi), a framework that allows
    to extract the CSI from a number of commercial Wi-Fi chipsets.

    For the experiments inherited from V1, the transmitter and receiver are both RT-AX82U 
    Access Points from Asus.
    For new experiments, the transmitter is an RT-AX82U and the receiver is an RT-AX86U
    from Asus. 
    All APs are equipped with the Wi-Fi Broadcom chipset BRCM43684.

2. Data processing:
    Raw CSI data have been processed with MATLAB and the complex-valued CSIs
    have been stored in the mat files that are made available. 
    CSI extraction from pcap files consists in the following steps, performed on each 
    collected packet:
    - extraction of metadata from the packet header;
    - extraction of the CSI from the packet payload: this task requires to account for all 
        the used spatial streams, depending on the number of antennas used at the transmitter
        and receiver; 
    - storage of the CSI in a mat file.

    To ensure the reproducibility of the results, the extracted CSI data are
    provided as well as the raw data.
    
3. Environmental conditions:
    The data collection has been performed over several months; however, the conditions
    of the room throughout the days are described in each config file associated to each
    set of experiments characterized by the same experimental setup.

    The different conditions under which the experiments were performed are a feature
    of the studies based on this dataset, as the goal of the papers associated to it is to
    characterize the long-term behavior of a propagation channel to support CSI-based 
    environment classification.

4. Privacy policy:
    The dataset does not contain any personal data. 
    The names of the people in the room at the time of each experiment have been 
    anonymized to guarantee their privacy.
    The CSI stored in the dataset have been collected from the nodes involved in the 
    experiment only and do not contain information related to any identifiable person.


For further information, we recommend to read the paper or contact the authors.

